    "difficulty": 3,
    "marks": 1,
    "estimatedSeconds": 75,
    "stem": "In triangle $ABC$, angle $A = 50^\\circ$ and angle $B = 70^\\circ$. In triangle $PQR$, angle $P = 50^\\circ$ and angle $R = 60^\\circ$. Which statement is correct?",
    "options": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "content": "The triangles are not similar, because angle $B$ is not equal to angle $R$.",
        "rationale": "Angles must be matched by size, not by position in the name: $B$ matches $Q$."
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "content": "The triangles are similar: both have angles $50^\\circ$, $70^\\circ$ and $60^\\circ$.",
        "correct": true,
        "rationale": "Angle $C = 60^\\circ$ and angle $Q = 70^\\circ$, so all three pairs of angles match ($A \\leftrightarrow P$, $B \\leftrightarrow Q$, $C \\leftrightarrow R$)."
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "content": "It is impossible to tell without knowing the side lengths.",
        "rationale": "Two pairs of equal angles are enough to prove similarity."
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "content": "The triangles are congruent.",
        "rationale": "Equal angles do not mean equal sides; the triangles may be different sizes."
      }
    ],
    "explanation": "The third angles are $C = 180^\\circ - 50^\\circ - 70^\\circ = 60^\\circ$ and $Q = 180^\\circ - 50^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 70^\\circ$. Both triangles have angles $50^\\circ$, $60^\\circ$ and $70^\\circ$, so they are similar. The corresponding vertices are $A \\leftrightarrow P$, $B \\leftrightarrow Q$ and $C \\leftrightarrow R$.",
    "hints": [
      "Work out the third angle of each triangle."
    ],
